
Understand Linux file types
Linux has different types of files.
Type	Meaning	Example
Regular file	Stores data or text	notes.txt
Directory	Contains files and directories	/home
Symbolic link	Points to another path	app-link
Device file	Represents a device	/dev/null
Named pipe	Used for process communication	FIFO

For this lesson, we mainly focus on regular text files.

Let's create our working directory:
mkdir -p ~/linux-lab/files
mkdir -p ~/linux-lab/logs
mkdir -p ~/linux-lab/config

cd ~/linux-lab


Part 2. Reading files
1. cat – Display file contents
cat stands for concatenate.

It is used to display file contents, combine files and write content through redirection.

First create a file:
cd ~/linux-lab/files

touch notes.txt


Add some content:
echo "Linux is an operating system" > notes.txt


Now display it:
cat notes.txt


Expected output:
Linux is an operating system


Important cat variations
Display line numbers:
cat -n notes.txt


Display multiple files:
cat file1.txt file2.txt


Combine files into another file:
cat file1.txt file2.txt > combined.txt


Real-world usage: Quickly inspect a small configuration file.

Example:
cat /etc/hostname


2. less – Read large files
Imagine a log file containing 100,000 lines.

Using cat will print everything into the terminal, making it difficult to inspect.

For this purpose, use less.
less notes.txt


Useful keyboard shortcuts:
Key	Purpose
Space	Next page
b	Previous page
Enter	Move one line down
g	Go to beginning
G	Go to end
/error	Search for error
n	Next search result
q	Quit

Company example:
less /var/log/syslog


You can inspect system logs page by page.

Remember:
- cat = Display the whole file.
- less = Read large files interactively.
3. head – Display beginning of file
Purpose: Displays the first lines of a file.
head notes.txt


By default, it displays 10 lines.

Display first 5 lines:
head -n 5 notes.txt


Example:
head -n 20 /var/log/syslog


Useful when checking the beginning of a log file.
4. tail – Display end of file
Purpose: Displays the last lines of a file.
tail notes.txt


Display last 5 lines:
tail -n 5 notes.txt


This is especially useful because application errors often appear near the end of log files.
5. tail -f – Monitor logs continuously
Important for Data Engineering
This is one of the most useful commands for Linux server troubleshooting.

Suppose a Spark application is running and continuously writing logs.

You want to watch the log file as new entries appear.

Use:
tail -f ~/linux-lab/logs/spark.log


The terminal remains active and displays new lines as they are added.

To stop monitoring:
Ctrl + C


Difference between tail and tail -f
Command	Behavior
tail file.txt	Displays the last lines and exits
tail -f file.txt	Continues monitoring new lines

Real-world example:
tail -f /var/log/syslog


This monitors new system log entries.

Important: Use tail -F when you also want tail to recover from a log file being rotated or recreated.

